{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>ומי</b> (and who). Always: the heh of the interrogative [has] shva and pataḥ, unless after it comes shva na and pataḥ, like המבחנים and the phrase הייטב בעיני ה׳. And after the definite heh [there is] a dagesh. And if after the heh [comes] one of the letters אחה״ע and pataḥ — [it is] the pataḥ of the interrogative heh, and sometimes [it is] in place of the definite heh, and the majority [is] qamatz. And the sage [Solomon] comes with small pataḥ because of the weight of the tongue, because after it come two qamatzim. And there is no difference between החכם עיניו בראשו and החכם יהיה. And in the plural [construct] form one opens [with pataḥ] the [vowel] of the interrogative heh and [that] of the definite heh [with] qamatz."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {0 "Resolved החכם as 'the sage [Solomon]' by inference from Ibn Ezra's standard usage. The final sentence regarding plural forms is textually dense; translated hyper-literally."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:37.508023780Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}